                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238919723",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561197972497151",
                            "num_reviews": 23,
                            "playtime_forever": 981,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 981,
                            "playtime_at_review": 719,
                            "last_played": 1793579120
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "There are [b]a lot[/b] of negative reviews on here which put me off from trying initially, but they were all about one thing: issues with controllers. I found that once I had faffed around changing all of the controller buttons and axes to one single key that I'd never use, the game worked quite well.\r\n\r\nYes, it's a pain, and it looks like they used a disgruntled intern to make the controller and key bindings part. BUT - once you've made it past that, the game gets intriguing.\r\n\r\nIf you've played the free demo, then you pretty much need to forget that as the game is fairly different. You pay in-game money to do training missions that are usually quite good and thorough, though sometimes they might get a label wrong. You can then do real missions to earn money, though you also have to pay for repairs or new parts yourself.\r\n\r\nInitial missions are just \"go there and repair that particular pipe\" whilst later missions are more \"something isn't working, go and find out what it is and fix it\" which is great fun.\r\n\r\nAnd the fixing and diagnostic elements genuinely are fun. Using multimeters, schematics, IR scanners, etc. to find and fix problems is a great puzzle game.\r\n\r\nSadly, the game seems mostly abandoned by the devs as they kept getting negative reviews and apparently didn't market their game to the right people. If they'd simply put in an option to disable controller inputs, most of their negative reviews wouldn't have been there in the first place.\r\n\r\nIf you like the idea of diagnosing problems in space, don't mind faffing with controller binds until they work, and don't mind putting up with very minor graphical and other glitches which will never get fixed, then this game is for you.",
